        "label": "Existing patch analysis detection strategy",
        "attributes": {
          "primary_pattern": "double_free",
          "check_types": [
            "error_path_cleanup_mismatch",
            "partial_initialization_resource_lifetime",
            "duplicate_release_of_aggregate_owned_resources",
            "replacement_of_broad_close_free_with_narrow_destroy"
          ],
          "entry_points": [
            "hws_send_ring_create_sq_rdy",
            "hws_send_ring_open_sq",
            "hws_send_ring_close_sq",
            "hws_send_ring_destroy_sq"
          ],
          "data_flow_tracking": true,
          "cross_file_analysis": false,
          "suggestions": [
            "First-draft detector should anchor on the patch-local old sink: an error branch after hws_send_ring_set_sq_rdy(mdev, sq->sqn) that calls hws_send_ring_close_sq(sq).",
            "Treat the added hws_send_ring_destroy_sq(mdev, sq) / mlx5_core_destroy_sq(mdev, sq->sqn) as the barrier replacement: it destroys only the core SQ handle instead of closing/freeing the whole sq object.",
            "Do not require detailed knowledge of sq->dep_wqe, sq->wq_ctrl.buf.frags, or sq->wr_priv for the first hit; those are useful secondary convergence filters if hws_send_ring_close_sq() is modeled as freeing them.",
            "A local CFG check in drivers/net/ethernet/mellanox/mlx5/core/steering/hws/mlx5hws_send.c is enough for the initial checker: match successful SQ creation, failed transition to ready state, then broad close on the same sq.",
            "Use interprocedural modeling only as a refinement to confirm that hws_send_ring_close_sq(sq) releases SQ-owned allocations also freed by the caller cleanup; the patch-local replacement itself is sufficient as the primary anchor.",
            "Avoid treating the new helper as merely a rename: its body calls mlx5_core_destroy_sq(mdev, sq->sqn), so the semantic distinction is narrower handle destruction versus broad object close."
          ]
        }
      },
